+          "claim": "Changing a representative $i_j$ by $N_j$ changes the exponent by the integer multiple $2\\pi\\mathrm i\\ell_j$, so [F2] makes $\\phi_\\ell$ well-defined on the periodic grid. It is nonzero because $\\phi_\\ell(0)=1$. Put $\\beta_j=2\\pi\\ell_j/N_j$. The addition law gives $\\phi_\\ell(i\\pm e_j)=e^{\\pm\\mathrm i\\beta_j}\\phi_\\ell(i)$, including wrapped indices. By [F2], $e^{\\mathrm i\\beta_j}$ has modulus one and $e^{\\mathrm i\\beta_j}e^{-\\mathrm i\\beta_j}=1$, hence $e^{-\\mathrm i\\beta_j}=\\overline{e^{\\mathrm i\\beta_j}}$. The Cartesian Euler formula in [F2] and [F3] therefore give $2-e^{\\mathrm i\\beta_j}-e^{-\\mathrm i\\beta_j}=2-2\\cos\\beta_j=4\\sin^2(\\beta_j/2)$. Substitution into [F1] yields the asserted eigenvalue on summing over $j$; it is nonnegative because each $\\kappa_j/h_j^2$ is positive and each sine square is nonnegative.",
