+          "claim": "Put $P=\\prod_{j=1}^dN_j>0$. By [F2], [F4] and the finite Fubini rule [F5], $\\sum_{i\\in G_h^{\\rm per}}\\phi_\\ell(i)\\overline{\\phi_m(i)}=\\prod_{j=1}^d\\sum_{r=0}^{N_j-1}\\exp(2\\pi\\mathrm i(\\ell_j-m_j)r/N_j)$. This is $P$ when $\\ell=m$ and zero otherwise: distinct index tuples have a coordinate whose two standard representatives are not congruent modulo $N_j$. Thus the nonzero modes are orthogonal, also after multiplying the pairing by the positive grid weight $\\prod_jh_j$. Apply the published one-dimensional inversion formula [F6] successively to each of the $d$ coordinates of any grid vector. All sums are finite, so [F5] combines these expansions into a linear combination of the displayed product modes $\\phi_\\ell$; hence they span the complexified grid space. If a linear combination of them is zero, multiply it by $\\overline{\\phi_m}$ and sum over the grid. The displayed pairing identity leaves $P$ times its $m$th coefficient, so every coefficient is zero. The modes therefore form a basis.",
